const lesson = (id, level, track, title, what, how, code, delivery, followups, answers, traps, realWorld) => ({id, level, topic: track, title, what, how, code, delivery, followups, followupAnswers: answers, traps, realWorld});

export const deepDiveLessons = [
lesson('deep-001','L2','Java Internals','How does HashMap actually find a value?','HashMap stores entries in buckets selected from a hash derived from the key. Equality is checked only after the candidate bucket is located.','hashCode is spread, masked into a bucket index, then entries are compared with equals. Heavy collisions can turn a bucket into a tree in modern Java.','int i = (n - 1) & hash(key.hashCode());\nNode<K,V> e = table[i];\nif (e != null && key.equals(e.key)) return e.value;','I explain HashMap as hashing plus equality: hash narrows the search, equals proves identity. Correct immutable keys and a stable hashCode/equals contract are critical.',['Why can a mutable key break a HashMap?','Why are hashCode and equals both needed?'],['If fields used by hashCode change after insertion, the entry may remain in the old bucket and become effectively unreachable.','hashCode chooses candidates efficiently; equals determines whether a candidate is actually the same logical key.'],['Do not say HashMap uses equals to find the bucket.','Do not mutate key state used by hashCode while it is stored.'],'Configuration maps, caches and lookup-heavy services depend on predictable key behavior.'),
lesson('deep-002','L2','Java Internals','What happens when an object becomes unreachable?','An object is eligible for garbage collection when it cannot be reached from GC roots such as live threads, static references or JNI references.','The collector traces reachability rather than reference counts. Depending on the collector, it marks reachable objects, reclaims or compacts memory, and may move objects.','Service s = new Service();\ns = null; // eligible only if no other path reaches it','I distinguish eligibility from immediate collection: setting a reference to null does not force GC; it only removes one reachability path.',['What is a GC root?','Can System.gc() guarantee collection?'],['Typical roots include active stack references, static fields, thread objects and native references.','No. System.gc() is only a request and the JVM may ignore it.'],['Do not equate out-of-scope objects with immediately freed memory.','Avoid using System.gc() as application memory management.'],'Production diagnosis uses heap dumps, allocation profiling and GC logs rather than manually forcing collection.'),
lesson('deep-003','L2','Java Concurrency','What does volatile guarantee?','volatile gives visibility and ordering guarantees for accesses to a field; it does not make compound operations such as count++ atomic.','A volatile write establishes a happens-before relationship with subsequent reads of that variable. For read-modify-write, use synchronization or an atomic class.','private volatile boolean running;\nwhile (running) { work(); }','I use volatile for state publication and flags, but I use AtomicInteger or locks when multiple steps must be indivisible.',['Is volatile enough for i++?','When would you use AtomicInteger?'],['No. The read, increment and write are separate operations.','When independent atomic updates are needed without a lock, such as counters or compare-and-set state.'],['Do not describe volatile as a universal thread-safety keyword.','Visibility is not the same as atomicity.'],'Shutdown flags, configuration publication and lightweight state coordination are common uses.'),
lesson('deep-004','L2','Java Concurrency','How does CompletableFuture compose asynchronous work?','CompletableFuture represents a result that may complete later and provides operators for transforming, combining and recovering from that result.','thenApply transforms a result, thenCompose flattens dependent async stages, allOf waits for multiple stages, and exceptionally/handle provide recovery.','CompletableFuture<User> u = loadUser();\nCompletableFuture<Order> o = u.thenCompose(this::loadOrder);','I choose composition based on dependency: thenCompose for dependent async work, thenCombine for independent results, and explicit timeouts for remote calls.',['thenApply vs thenCompose?','What is a common production mistake?'],['thenApply maps a value to another value; thenCompose maps it to another CompletionStage and flattens the chain.','Blocking with get/join inside request threads can destroy the benefit and exhaust pools.'],['Do not call async code automatically non-blocking end-to-end.','Always reason about executor choice and downstream latency.'],'Remote fan-out and parallel enrichment can be composed while keeping failure handling explicit.'),
lesson('deep-005','L2','Spring Internals','What really happens when Spring creates a bean?','The container resolves bean definitions, instantiates objects, injects dependencies, runs post-processors and lifecycle callbacks, then exposes the bean for use.','BeanFactory/PostProcessor phases can inspect or wrap beans. AOP commonly creates proxies around eligible beans rather than modifying the target class directly.','@Service\nclass PaymentService { }\n// discovered → instantiated → dependencies injected → post-processed','I explain the container as an object lifecycle, not magic annotation scanning. This helps diagnose circular dependencies, proxy behavior and initialization failures.',['Where does @Autowired happen?','Why can a proxy matter?'],['Dependency injection is performed during bean creation by the container, according to the injection metadata.','Calls crossing a Spring proxy can trigger transactions, security, caching or other advice.'],['Do not assume every call on this inside the same object passes through the proxy.','Do not hide complex initialization in constructors.'],'Understanding lifecycle order is useful for startup failures and AOP surprises.'),
lesson('deep-006','L2','Spring Transactions','How does @Transactional actually work?','Spring usually applies transaction behavior through an AOP proxy that starts, commits or rolls back a transaction around a method invocation.','The proxy intercepts the call, obtains transaction resources, invokes the target and commits or rolls back according to the configured rules. Self-invocation bypasses the proxy.','@Transactional\npublic void transfer(...) { debit(); credit(); }','I describe the proxy boundary first, then propagation, isolation and rollback rules. The annotation alone is not the transaction; the interception and transaction manager are.',['Why does self-invocation matter?','What is the default rollback behavior?'],['A direct this.method() call does not cross the transactional proxy, so the advice may not run.','By default Spring rolls back for unchecked RuntimeException and Error, not every checked exception.'],['Do not assume @Transactional works on every internal method call.','Do not make remote HTTP calls inside long database transactions without a reason.'],'Transaction boundaries should protect database consistency while remaining short enough to avoid lock and connection pressure.'),
lesson('deep-007','L2','Spring Web','How does a Spring Boot request reach a controller?','An HTTP request enters the servlet stack, passes filters/security, is mapped by Spring MVC to a controller method, then the response is serialized.','DispatcherServlet coordinates handler mapping and invocation. Filters run around the servlet; HandlerInterceptors and controller advice participate at later MVC stages.','HTTP → Filter chain → DispatcherServlet → HandlerMapping → Controller → JSON','I trace a request boundary-by-boundary because it tells me where authentication, logging, validation and exception translation occur.',['Where should authentication happen?','Where are validation errors handled?'],['Authentication belongs in the security filter chain before protected controller logic.','Bean validation can fail before controller execution and be translated centrally with @ControllerAdvice.'],['Do not put security decisions only in the React client.','Do not duplicate exception formatting in every controller.'],'This model makes 401/403, validation, CORS and global error handling easier to diagnose.'),
lesson('deep-008','L2','JPA/Hibernate','Why does Hibernate sometimes issue unexpected SQL?','Hibernate manages an entity persistence context and synchronizes dirty managed state with the database, often at flush/commit rather than at the setter call.','Dirty checking compares managed state, flush triggers SQL generation, and lazy associations may issue additional queries when accessed.','@Transactional\nOrder o = em.find(Order.class,id);\no.setStatus(SHIPPED);\n// UPDATE is generated during flush','I separate object state from database state. A Java setter changes the managed entity; Hibernate decides when SQL is necessary.',['What is the persistence context?','When does flush happen?'],['It is the first-level cache and identity scope for managed entities inside a persistence context.','Common points are transaction commit and explicit flush; queries can also trigger an auto-flush depending on flush mode.'],['Do not assume save() immediately means SQL was sent.','Do not access lazy relations blindly outside the intended transaction boundary.'],'This explains duplicate selects, stale state, flush timing and transaction-related LazyInitializationException.'),
lesson('deep-009','L2','SQL Internals','How does an index improve a query?','An index provides an alternate ordered or searchable structure that can locate qualifying rows without scanning the whole table.','The optimizer compares access paths using statistics, selectivity, estimated cost and available indexes. A good index can still be ignored when a scan is cheaper.','CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_status\nON orders(customer_id, status);','I never say indexes always make queries faster. They speed selected reads but add write, storage and maintenance cost, and the optimizer decides whether to use them.',['Why can a query ignore an index?','Why does column order matter?'],['Low selectivity, stale statistics, functions on columns, type mismatches or a cheaper full scan can make an index unattractive.','A composite index is ordered by its leading columns, so predicates and sort patterns should match the access path.'],['Do not create one index per column blindly.','Check the actual execution plan before changing indexes.'],'Production tuning starts with the real query, cardinality and execution plan.'),
lesson('deep-010','L2','SQL Transactions','What is MVCC and why does it matter?','Multi-version concurrency control lets transactions observe consistent row versions while allowing many reads and writes to proceed with less blocking.','The database tracks transaction visibility/version information and determines which row version is visible to a statement or transaction according to isolation semantics.','BEGIN;\nSELECT balance FROM account WHERE id=1;\n-- concurrent updates can coexist according to isolation level','I connect MVCC to isolation rather than treating it as a magic performance feature: visibility rules determine what a transaction can see.',['Does MVCC remove locking?','How does isolation change behavior?'],['No. Databases still use locks or other coordination for writes and conflict control.','Higher isolation can prevent anomalies but may increase contention, retries or serialization failures.'],['Do not say MVCC means there are no locks.','Do not choose SERIALIZABLE without understanding workload and retry behavior.'],'MVCC is central to PostgreSQL behavior under concurrent API traffic.'),
lesson('deep-011','L2','Microservices','Why is a distributed transaction harder than a local transaction?','A local transaction can atomically commit one database resource; a distributed workflow spans independent services and failure can occur between steps.','Instead of assuming global ACID, services often use local transactions plus events, sagas, idempotency and compensating actions.','Order DB: PENDING → event → Payment → event → Shipment\nFailure → compensation/retry','I model the business invariant first and then choose coordination. A saga is a workflow of local transactions, not a distributed database transaction.',['What if payment succeeds but the order update fails?','Why is idempotency required?'],['Persist durable state and retry the event/update; design the consumer so repeating the operation does not double-charge or corrupt state.','At-least-once delivery and retries can repeat messages, so the operation needs a stable deduplication key or idempotent effect.'],['Do not claim Kafka or HTTP retries create exactly-once business behavior.','Do not use compensation when the business action is irreversible without a real recovery plan.'],'Distributed consistency is a business workflow problem as much as a technical one.'),
lesson('deep-012','L2','Microservices Resilience','How should timeouts and retries interact?','A timeout bounds how long a caller waits; a retry repeats an operation after a failure, but retries can amplify load and duplicate side effects.','Set deadlines, retry only transient failures, use bounded exponential backoff with jitter, and cap attempts. Propagate the remaining deadline downstream.','deadline = 2s\nretry: 100ms, 250ms, 500ms + jitter\nstop when deadline expires','I treat the end-to-end deadline as the budget. Each downstream call must consume only part of it; retries are conditional and bounded.',['Why add jitter?','Should POST always be retried?'],['Jitter prevents many clients from retrying simultaneously after the same outage.','Only if the operation is explicitly idempotent or protected by an idempotency key/semantic guarantee.'],['Do not retry every 500 or timeout automatically.','Do not let nested services each perform three retries and multiply traffic.'],'Retry storms are a common cause of cascading failure.'),
lesson('deep-013','L2','Kafka Internals','What is a Kafka partition really giving you?','A partition is an ordered append-only log segment within a topic and the unit of parallel consumption and ordering.','Records in one partition have a stable order; consumer groups divide partitions among consumers, so ordering is guaranteed per partition rather than globally across a topic.','topic=orders\npartition-0: offset 10,11,12\npartition-1: offset 20,21','I explain partitions as the trade-off between scale and ordering. More partitions increase parallelism but do not create global ordering.',['How is a record assigned to a partition?','Can two consumers read the same partition in one group?'],['Usually by key hashing when a key is supplied, otherwise partition selection can distribute records.','Not concurrently as active owners; one partition is assigned to one consumer within a consumer group at a time.'],['Do not promise topic-wide ordering.','Do not increase partitions without considering key distribution and ordering requirements.'],'Partition count affects throughput, consumer parallelism and future scaling choices.'),
lesson('deep-014','L2','Kafka Delivery','Why is exactly-once difficult at the business level?','Kafka can provide strong processing guarantees for specific Kafka-to-Kafka workflows, but external side effects such as database writes require separate coordination.','Transactions can atomically publish consumed offsets and produced Kafka records, while an external database still needs an outbox/idempotency strategy or coordinated transaction.','DB transaction → outbox row\nrelay → Kafka\nconsumer → idempotent DB effect','I separate transport semantics from business effects. “Exactly once” must be defined across the actual boundaries involved.',['How does the outbox help?','What does at-least-once mean for consumers?'],['The business event is stored in the same local DB transaction as the state change, so a relay can publish it reliably later.','A message may be delivered more than once, so the consumer must tolerate duplicates.'],['Do not equate Kafka EOS with exactly-once database side effects.','Do not delete the outbox before reliable publication is established.'],'The outbox pattern closes the dual-write gap between a database and an event broker.'),
lesson('deep-015','L2','React Rendering','What causes a React component to re-render?','A component renders again when its state changes, its parent renders with relevant props, or a consumed context value changes.','React builds a new element tree, compares it with the previous tree, and commits necessary DOM updates. Memoization can reduce renders but is not a correctness mechanism.','const [count,setCount] = useState(0);\nsetCount(count + 1); // schedules a render','I distinguish render from DOM mutation: rendering calculates the next UI; commit applies required changes.',['Does every render update the DOM?','When does React.memo help?'],['No. React can render and then determine that no host changes are necessary.','When a component receives the same props frequently and its render work is meaningful enough to justify comparison.'],['Do not treat re-render as DOM replacement.','Do not add memo everywhere without measuring.'],'This distinction is important for React performance debugging.'),
lesson('deep-016','L2','JavaScript Runtime','Why does the event loop make async JavaScript non-blocking?','JavaScript executes synchronous code on a call stack while the host environment handles timers, network and other asynchronous operations, later queuing callbacks.','After the stack is empty, the runtime drains microtasks before moving through task queues. Long synchronous work still blocks the main thread.','console.log("A");\nPromise.resolve().then(()=>console.log("microtask"));\nsetTimeout(()=>console.log("task"));\nconsole.log("B");','I explain the event loop as scheduling, not parallel execution of JavaScript. Async APIs avoid blocking while the callback waits for the host operation.',['Which runs first: Promise callback or setTimeout?','Can async code still freeze the UI?'],['A resolved Promise callback is a microtask and normally runs before the next timer task.','Yes. A long synchronous loop blocks the main thread and prevents rendering and input processing.'],['Do not call JavaScript single-threaded equivalent to “nothing happens concurrently.”','Do not use synchronous CPU-heavy work on the browser main thread.'],'This matters for React rendering, input latency and API response handling.'),
lesson('deep-017','L2','Docker Internals','Why are Docker image layers useful?','An image is composed of immutable filesystem layers; containers add a writable layer on top.','Build steps can reuse unchanged layers from cache. Changing an early Dockerfile instruction can invalidate later layers, so ordering affects build speed.','COPY package.json ./\nRUN npm ci\nCOPY src ./src','I order Dockerfile steps from stable to volatile so dependency installation can stay cached when source code changes.',['Does a container modify the image?','Why avoid copying secrets into a layer?'],['The container writes to its own writable layer; the image layers remain immutable.','Secrets can remain in image history/layers even after deleting the file in a later layer.'],['Do not treat rm in a later layer as secure secret removal.','Do not put build credentials into Dockerfile ARG/ENV casually.'],'Layering affects build performance, storage reuse and supply-chain risk.'),
lesson('deep-018','L2','Kubernetes Control Plane','How does a Kubernetes Deployment create Pods?','A Deployment declares desired application state and manages ReplicaSets, which create and maintain the requested number of Pods.','The controller compares desired and observed state, creates/updates a ReplicaSet and the ReplicaSet creates Pods through the API server/scheduler/kubelet path.','Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod\nAPI server → scheduler → kubelet','I explain Kubernetes as a reconciliation system: controllers continuously move observed state toward declared state.',['What happens if a Pod dies?','Who actually starts the container?'],['The ReplicaSet creates a replacement Pod to restore the desired replica count.','The kubelet on the selected node asks the container runtime to create/start containers.'],['Do not say the Deployment directly starts containers.','Do not confuse the control plane with node-level execution.'],'The reconciliation model explains self-healing and rollout behavior.'),
lesson('deep-019','L2','Kubernetes Networking','Why can a Service survive Pod replacement?','A Service provides a stable virtual endpoint while its selector identifies currently ready backend Pods.','Endpoints/EndpointSlices are updated as Pods become eligible, so clients target the Service rather than individual Pod IPs.','client → Service:8080 → Pod A\nPod A dies → Pod C becomes endpoint','I use Services to decouple clients from ephemeral Pod identities. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive traffic.',['Why can a Service have no endpoints?','Does a Service itself run a process?'],['Selector mismatch, labels, readiness or namespace/port configuration can result in no endpoints.','Usually no; it is an abstraction implemented by cluster networking components.'],['Do not debug a 503 by checking only the Pod process.','Verify selectors, endpoints and target ports together.'],'Service/endpoints separation is a key Kubernetes debugging concept.'),
lesson('deep-020','L2','Kubernetes Scheduling','Why does a Pod remain Pending?','Pending means Kubernetes has not yet reached a running/scheduled state for the Pod; the scheduler may be unable to find a suitable node or another dependency may block progress.','Inspect kubectl describe pod events, resource requests, taints/tolerations, node selectors, affinity and PVC binding.','kubectl describe pod api-0\nkubectl get nodes\nkubectl get pvc','I start with scheduler events because they usually state the concrete constraint rather than guessing at the application.',['How do resource requests affect scheduling?','What does a taint do?'],['The scheduler reserves a Pod based on requested resources, not just current instantaneous usage.','A taint repels Pods unless they have a matching toleration.'],['Do not increase node count blindly.','Do not confuse limits with requests during scheduling.'],'Pending is usually a placement or dependency problem, not an application-code problem.'),
lesson('deep-021','L2','CI/CD Design','Why should a pipeline produce immutable artifacts?','An immutable artifact is built once and promoted across environments without rebuilding different bytes for each environment.','Build from a known commit, record a version/digest, scan and test it, then deploy the same artifact through environments with configuration supplied separately.','commit abc → image sha256:123 → staging → production','I want promotion to change where an artifact runs, not what was built. This improves traceability and rollback confidence.',['Why not rebuild for production?','Where should environment config live?'],['A rebuild can change dependencies, timestamps or build inputs and make the tested artifact differ from production.','Outside the artifact, using controlled configuration/secrets appropriate to the environment.'],['Do not tag only as latest for production traceability.','Do not bake environment secrets into the image.'],'Immutable promotion makes “what exactly is running?” answerable during incidents.'),
lesson('deep-022','L2','GitHub Actions','How should OIDC replace long-lived cloud keys?','OIDC lets a workflow obtain short-lived cloud credentials based on an identity token and cloud trust policy.','The workflow requests an OIDC token; the cloud identity provider validates claims such as repository/ref/environment, then issues temporary credentials with scoped permissions.','GitHub job → OIDC token → cloud STS → temporary role credentials','I prefer workload identity over storing permanent access keys because the credential lifetime and trust conditions are constrained.',['What claims should you restrict?','Is OIDC itself authorization?'],['Restrict repository, branch/tag, workflow or environment claims as supported by the cloud trust policy.','No. OIDC establishes identity; the cloud role policy determines what that identity can do.'],['Do not trust any repository if production credentials are involved.','Do not grant the role broader permissions than deployment requires.'],'This is a major production security improvement for CI/CD.'),
lesson('deep-023','L2','Jenkins','Why are Jenkins agents important?','Agents provide execution environments for pipeline steps, separating controller orchestration from build workloads.','Labels or pipeline configuration route work to suitable agents. Ephemeral agents can provide clean environments and reduce cross-build contamination.','pipeline {\n  agent { label "docker" }\n  stages { ... }\n}','I keep the controller focused on orchestration and use purpose-built, preferably ephemeral agents for builds and deployments.',['Why use ephemeral agents?','What should never be trusted from a workspace?'],['They reduce state leakage between builds and make tool versions reproducible.','Generated files and downloaded dependencies can be attacker-controlled; clean workspaces and explicit trust boundaries are important.'],['Do not run every workload on one persistent agent.','Do not assume workspace contents are trusted just because Jenkins created the directory.'],'Agent isolation is part of both reliability and CI security.'),
lesson('deep-024','L2','Cloud Networking','How does a request reach a private Kubernetes workload in AWS?','A public edge endpoint can accept client traffic while routing to private subnets where application workloads run, with security controls at each hop.','DNS resolves to an edge/load balancer, listener rules route to targets/ingress, network routes permit the path, and security groups/NACLs enforce allowed flows.','Internet → DNS → ALB → private subnet → ingress/service → Pod','I draw the packet path and validate DNS, route tables, listener targets and security groups in that order.',['Why put workloads in private subnets?','What is a security group?'],['It reduces direct internet exposure; only intended edge components need public reachability.','A stateful virtual firewall attached to supported resources that controls allowed inbound/outbound traffic.'],['Do not call private subnet synonymous with secure.','Check return paths and DNS as well as inbound rules.'],'Network debugging is easier when every hop has a concrete endpoint and policy.'),
lesson('deep-025','L2','Observability','Why are logs, metrics and traces complementary?','Logs provide event detail, metrics show aggregate behavior over time, and traces show a request’s path and latency across distributed components.','Correlate them with service, environment, version and trace/request identifiers. Use metrics for detection, traces for path analysis and logs for detailed evidence.','metric: http_server_requests_total\ntrace: abc123\nlog: traceId=abc123 status=500','I do not ask “which tool?” first. I ask what evidence is missing: trend, request path or event detail.',['Can logs replace metrics?','What makes a trace useful?'],['They are poor at aggregation and alerting at scale compared with purpose-built metrics.','Consistent propagation, meaningful spans, timestamps and useful attributes without leaking sensitive data.'],['Do not put high-cardinality user data into every metric label.','Do not log secrets or raw credentials for debugging.'],'Good observability shortens the time from symptom to failing boundary.'),
lesson('deep-026','L3','System Design','How do you choose synchronous HTTP vs asynchronous messaging?','HTTP is useful when the caller needs an immediate response and simple request/response semantics; messaging is useful when work can be decoupled, buffered or retried asynchronously.','Choose based on latency, coupling, failure handling, ordering, durability and consumer independence rather than fashion.','checkout request → HTTP validation\norder created → event → async fulfillment','I start with the business interaction. If the user needs an immediate decision, synchronous is natural; if downstream work can happen later, an event can reduce coupling.',['Can messaging make everything more reliable?','When is synchronous simpler?'],['No. It introduces broker operations, eventual consistency and duplicate/replay concerns.','For short local workflows with one clear owner and immediate feedback, HTTP can be easier to reason about.'],['Do not introduce Kafka just to avoid a REST call.','Do not hide user-visible latency behind an async workflow without a clear status model.'],'Architecture should optimize the actual constraint, not technology popularity.'),
lesson('deep-027','L3','System Design','How do you design idempotency for a payment API?','Idempotency lets repeated requests with the same client-generated key produce one business effect rather than multiple charges.','Persist the key with request/result state under a uniqueness constraint, process the business operation transactionally, and return the stored result for repeats.','POST /payments\nIdempotency-Key: 7f...\nunique(payment_request.key)','I make idempotency a durable server-side property. A frontend button guard alone cannot protect against retries, timeouts or duplicate delivery.',['Where should the key be stored?','What if the first request is still processing?'],['Store it in durable storage with enough scope and retention to cover the business retry window.','Return an in-progress response or safely coordinate on the same key; never execute the charge twice.'],['Do not use an in-memory map for a multi-instance API.','Do not reuse one key for different business requests.'],'Payments, order creation and webhook handling are classic idempotency cases.'),
lesson('deep-028','L3','System Design','How do you design cursor pagination for a changing dataset?','Cursor pagination uses a stable ordering key to request the next slice without relying on an ever-growing offset.','Order by a unique composite such as created_at,id and encode the last seen values into an opaque cursor. Query with a seek predicate.','SELECT ... FROM orders\nWHERE (created_at,id) < (?,?)\nORDER BY created_at DESC,id DESC\nLIMIT 50','I prefer cursor pagination for large or frequently changing datasets because it avoids deep offset scans and reduces page drift.',['Why include a unique tie-breaker?','Should the cursor expose database IDs?'],['Equal timestamps need deterministic ordering, so a unique second key prevents duplicates/skips between pages.','Prefer an opaque signed/encoded cursor so clients depend on the contract rather than storage details.'],['Do not use non-deterministic ordering.','Do not claim cursors automatically solve all consistency problems.'],'Feeds, audit logs and large admin tables benefit from seek-based pagination.'),
lesson('deep-029','L3','Performance','How do you diagnose a slow endpoint systematically?','Start with measured latency and break it into client, edge, application, downstream and database components before optimizing.','Use request traces, application timing, database plans/slow-query data, CPU/memory/thread-pool metrics and dependency latency. Form one hypothesis at a time.','p95 1.8s\ntrace: controller 50ms + DB 1.5s + serialization 20ms','I avoid “optimize everything.” I locate the dominant latency contributor, verify it with evidence, change one thing, and compare before/after measurements.',['What if CPU is low but latency is high?','Why inspect p95 instead of only average?'],['The service may be waiting on I/O, locks, connection pools, downstream calls or thread starvation.','Tail latency exposes slow requests that averages can hide and that users still experience.'],['Do not add cache before identifying the bottleneck.','Do not trust one slow request as a capacity conclusion.'],'This method applies across React, Spring, databases, Kubernetes and cloud infrastructure.'),
lesson('deep-030','L3','Reliability','What is backpressure?','Backpressure is a mechanism that prevents producers from overwhelming a slower consumer or resource.','Bound queues, limit concurrency, apply admission control, use broker retention and consumer lag, and shed or defer work when capacity is exhausted.','incoming=1000/s\nworker capacity=300/s\n→ queue grows unless admission is bounded','I treat an unbounded queue as hidden memory. The system needs a deliberate response when arrival rate exceeds service rate.',['Where can backpressure exist in a web system?','What happens without it?'],['HTTP connection pools, thread pools, Kafka consumer lag, bounded executors and database connection pools are examples.','Latency and memory grow until timeouts, OOM or cascading failure occur.'],['Do not solve overload with unlimited threads.','Do not let queues grow forever without a capacity policy.'],'Backpressure connects application concurrency with infrastructure capacity.'),
lesson('deep-031','L3','Security','How should authorization be enforced in a full-stack system?','Authorization is a server-side decision about whether an authenticated principal may perform a specific action on a resource.','React can hide controls for UX, but Spring Security or service-layer policy must enforce roles, scopes, ownership and business rules on the server.','DELETE /orders/42\n→ authenticate user\n→ authorize order ownership/role\n→ execute','I never trust the UI for authorization. The server receives an untrusted request and evaluates the current principal against the resource and action.',['Role vs permission?','Where should ownership checks happen?'],['Roles group permissions; fine-grained authorization often uses permissions/scopes plus resource attributes.','At the server boundary where the resource and authenticated identity are available, often service/policy code.'],['Do not authorize solely by a hidden React button.','Do not expose predictable IDs and assume that authentication implies ownership.'],'This prevents IDOR-style access bugs and keeps security decisions close to business rules.'),
lesson('deep-032','L3','Security','Why should JWT validation check more than the signature?','A valid signature proves the token was signed by a trusted issuer/key, but claims such as issuer, audience, expiry and algorithm also determine whether it is valid for this service.','Validate signature against trusted keys and enforce expected issuer, audience, temporal claims and acceptable algorithms. Then map claims to authorities.','JWT → signature → iss → aud → exp/nbf → authorities','I separate cryptographic validity from application acceptance. A token can be correctly signed and still be intended for another audience or already expired.',['Where do keys come from?','Should every service call the identity provider?'],['Usually from a trusted JWKS endpoint with caching and key rotation support.','No. Services can validate locally using cached public keys; introspection is another model with different trade-offs.'],['Do not accept any issuer just because the signature verifies.','Do not disable audience/expiry checks to “fix” authentication failures.'],'JWT validation belongs at a trust boundary and should fail closed.'),
lesson('deep-033','L3','Security','How do you prevent secrets from leaking through CI/CD?','Secrets should be injected only where needed, have limited scope and lifetime, and never be committed, printed or embedded into artifacts.','Use cloud workload identity/OIDC, secret stores, masked variables and short-lived credentials. Audit workflow logs and artifact contents.','GitHub Actions → OIDC → temporary role\nsecret manager → runtime env','I design secret flow end-to-end: source, transport, use, logs, storage and rotation. “Masked” does not make an exposed secret safe.',['What if a secret appears in logs?','Should secrets be in Docker ENV?'],['Revoke/rotate it immediately, investigate exposure and remove it from logs/artifacts where possible.','Runtime injection can be acceptable, but build-time image layers and logs must not persist sensitive values.'],['Do not paste secrets into workflow YAML.','Do not rely only on log masking.'],'Supply-chain security is partly about controlling where credentials can travel.'),
lesson('deep-034','L3','Distributed Systems','What does eventual consistency mean for an API?','A read may temporarily return older state because replicas or downstream services converge after a write rather than becoming globally visible atomically.','Expose explicit state transitions, use read-your-writes where needed, query the authoritative source for critical decisions, or make the UI tolerant of intermediate states.','POST /orders → 202 Accepted\nstatus: PROCESSING\nGET /orders/1 → READY later','I model consistency as a user-visible contract. The client should know whether a result is final, pending or stale.',['When is eventual consistency acceptable?','How can you improve read-after-write behavior?'],['When temporary staleness does not violate a business invariant, such as asynchronous search indexing.','Route the immediate read to the writer/leader or use a session/version token until replication catches up.'],['Do not hide eventual consistency behind a misleading 200 “final” response.','Do not use stale replicas for authorization or financial decisions.'],'Explicit state machines make asynchronous workflows understandable to users and operators.'),
lesson('deep-035','L3','Kubernetes Reliability','How do readiness and liveness differ?','Readiness controls whether traffic should be sent to a Pod; liveness determines whether Kubernetes should restart a container that is unhealthy.','Readiness can fail during dependency or startup conditions without restarting the process. Liveness should represent a condition where restarting is likely to recover the workload.','readiness: /actuator/health/readiness\nliveness: /actuator/health/liveness','I keep probes aligned to recovery semantics: readiness removes bad instances from traffic; liveness should not punish slow startup or transient downstream failure.',['What about startupProbe?','Can liveness cause an outage?'],['startupProbe gives slow-starting applications time before liveness/readiness probing becomes active.','Yes. An overly aggressive liveness probe can restart healthy-but-busy processes and create a restart loop.'],['Do not make liveness depend on every downstream service.','Do not use a probe that is more expensive than the health it measures.'],'Probe design directly affects availability during deploys and dependency failures.'),
lesson('deep-036','L3','Kubernetes Scaling','How does HPA decide to add Pods?','Horizontal Pod Autoscaler adjusts replica count toward a target metric such as CPU utilization or a custom metric.','It periodically observes metrics, compares current value with target, applies scaling bounds and stabilization behavior, and changes the workload replica count.','desiredReplicas ≈ currentReplicas × currentMetric / targetMetric','I treat HPA as a feedback controller, not instant capacity magic. Metrics, requests, startup time and workload shape determine whether scaling helps.',['Why do CPU-based HPAs need requests?','Why might scaling be too slow?'],['CPU utilization is commonly calculated relative to requested CPU, so bad requests distort the signal.','Metric collection, evaluation intervals, cold starts and traffic ramp can all add delay.'],['Do not set CPU requests arbitrarily low just to trigger HPA.','Do not expect HPA to fix a database bottleneck.'],'Scaling one tier can simply move the bottleneck to another tier.'),
lesson('deep-037','L3','Cloud Databases','How do connection pools cause database outages?','A connection pool limits how many application threads can hold database connections. Exhaustion makes requests wait, increasing latency and potentially causing a timeout cascade.','Size pools against database capacity and total service replicas. Monitor active, idle, pending and timeout metrics, and keep transactions short.','pods=20 × pool=20 = 400 possible DB connections','I calculate pool capacity across replicas rather than looking at one Pod. A “larger pool” can overload the database instead of improving throughput.',['What if the pool is exhausted?','How do you choose pool size?'],['Requests wait for a connection until timeout; inspect slow queries, transaction duration and leaked/unreleased resources.','Start from database capacity and workload concurrency, then validate with load tests and metrics.'],['Do not set maxPoolSize to a huge number by default.','Do not ignore replica count when calculating total connections.'],'Database capacity is a shared constraint across horizontally scaled application instances.'),
lesson('deep-038','L3','Observability','How do SLOs change alerting?','An SLO defines a target level of service over a time window, such as availability or latency, and an error budget is the allowed failure amount.','Alerts should focus on meaningful user-impacting budget consumption rather than every low-level anomaly. Burn-rate alerts can detect fast and slow budget exhaustion.','SLO=99.9%\nerror budget=0.1%\nfast burn + slow burn alerts','I use SLOs to connect engineering signals to user expectations. A CPU alert may be useful diagnostically, but budget burn tells me whether reliability is actually being consumed.',['SLA vs SLO?','What is an error budget for?'],['SLO is an internal reliability target; SLA is a customer/business commitment with consequences.','It provides a quantitative trade-off between reliability work and feature delivery.'],['Do not set 100% SLOs casually.','Do not page on every metric threshold without user impact.'],'SLOs help teams prioritize reliability work objectively.'),
lesson('deep-039','L3','Production Debugging','How do you debug a 502 from an ingress/load balancer?','A 502 usually means the proxy could not successfully communicate with a backend target or received an invalid upstream response.','Trace the path: listener rule → target/Ingress → Service → endpoints → Pod readiness → container port → application logs. Check timeouts and protocol mismatches.','curl -v https://api.example.com/health\nkubectl get ingress,svc,endpoints\nkubectl logs deploy/api','I debug the network path one hop at a time. The fastest evidence is often whether the Service has healthy endpoints and whether the Pod is actually listening on the expected port.',['502 vs 503?','What if the Pod is healthy?'],['Exact semantics depend on the proxy, but 503 often indicates no usable backend while 502 often indicates an upstream communication/protocol failure.','Check service port/targetPort, protocol, TLS expectations, network policy and proxy health checks.'],['Do not restart every Pod before checking routing.','Do not assume HTTP 200 on localhost proves the proxy can reach the Pod.'],'Layered path debugging prevents random restarts.'),
lesson('deep-040','L3','Production Debugging','How do you debug a slow SQL query that appeared after a release?','Compare the query plan and runtime before/after the release and identify whether data volume, SQL shape, indexes, locks or application behavior changed.','Capture the exact query, parameters and execution plan. Check row estimates, scan type, join strategy, timing, locks and recent schema/index changes.','EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT ...;','I correlate the regression with the release, but I still validate it with the database plan rather than assuming the code change is the cause.',['Why can estimates be wrong?','What if the plan is fine?'],['Stale statistics, skewed data or correlated columns can make cardinality estimates inaccurate.','Look at lock waits, connection pool pressure, network time and application-side N+1 queries.'],['Do not add an index without understanding the query pattern.','Do not run ANALYZE/EXPLAIN ANALYZE blindly on critical production workloads without considering impact.'],'Database regressions often surface as application latency rather than obvious database errors.'),
lesson('deep-041','L3','React Performance','How do you diagnose a React page that feels slow?','Measure whether the bottleneck is network, JavaScript execution, React rendering, browser layout/paint or excessive data.','Use Network and Performance panels plus React profiling, then optimize the measured hotspot: request waterfalls, large bundles, repeated renders, expensive calculations or unnecessary DOM work.','Network → Performance → React Profiler\nmeasure → change → compare','I do not start with useMemo. I first identify whether the user is waiting for bytes, CPU, rendering or server latency.',['When is code splitting useful?','What can cause repeated renders?'],['It reduces initial JavaScript work by loading route/feature code when needed.','State changes, parent renders, context updates and unstable props can contribute.'],['Do not optimize based on component count alone.','Do not trade correctness for memoization.'],'Frontend performance is a full request-to-paint problem.'),
lesson('deep-042','L3','JavaScript Async','How do AbortController and request cancellation help?','AbortController lets application code signal that an asynchronous operation should be cancelled when its result is no longer useful.','Pass the signal to fetch and abort on component cleanup or when a newer request supersedes an older one.','const c = new AbortController();\nfetch(url,{signal:c.signal});\nc.abort();','I use cancellation to prevent stale responses and wasted work, especially for search-as-you-type and route changes.',['Does abort undo server-side work?','What should the UI do on AbortError?'],['Not necessarily; it stops the client-side request/processing path, while the server may already have executed the operation.','Treat intentional cancellation as a normal control flow rather than showing it as a user-facing failure.'],['Do not confuse cancellation with transactional rollback.','Do not leave controllers alive across obsolete requests.'],'Cancellation is complementary to debouncing; debounce reduces calls while abort prevents stale in-flight work.'),
lesson('deep-043','L3','Docker Production','Why should containers run as non-root?','A non-root process limits the impact if an application is compromised inside the container.','Create/use an unprivileged UID, ensure filesystem permissions match it, and drop unnecessary Linux capabilities.','FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre\nUSER 10001\nENTRYPOINT ["java","-jar","app.jar"]','I treat container identity as one layer of defense, not a substitute for Kubernetes or cloud isolation.',['What breaks when switching to non-root?','Are containers security boundaries?'],['Write permissions, bind mounts, temporary directories and ports below 1024 can expose assumptions that must be fixed explicitly.','They provide isolation but share the host kernel, so kernel/runtime vulnerabilities and excessive privileges still matter.'],['Do not run as root because it is convenient.','Do not grant privileged mode to fix filesystem issues casually.'],'Least privilege should exist at image, Pod and cloud identity layers.'),
lesson('deep-044','L3','Docker Networking','How does Docker Compose service discovery work?','Compose creates a network where services can reach each other by service name through internal DNS.','The application should use the service name and container port for internal traffic; published host ports are primarily for host/external access.','api → http://postgres:5432\nnot http://localhost:5432','I separate host networking from container networking. “localhost” inside the API container means the API container itself, not PostgreSQL.',['Why does localhost fail between containers?','When do you publish a port?'],['Each container has its own network namespace, so localhost points to itself.','Publish ports when a host/external client needs access; internal services can use the private Compose network.'],['Do not use host ports for every internal dependency.','Do not put databases on 0.0.0.0 unnecessarily.'],'This is a frequent local-to-container migration bug.'),
lesson('deep-045','L3','CI/CD Reliability','How do you prevent flaky tests from poisoning a pipeline?','A flaky test passes and fails without a meaningful product change, making pipeline status less trustworthy.','Capture evidence, isolate the test, identify shared state/timing/concurrency causes, fix the root issue and track quarantine explicitly if temporary suppression is unavoidable.','test → failure evidence → reproduce → root cause → fix\nquarantine only with owner + expiry','I never normalize flaky tests as “just rerun it.” Reruns can diagnose nondeterminism but should not become the reliability strategy.',['Why are retries dangerous for tests?','What are common flake causes?'],['Retries hide signal and allow broken tests to pass without understanding the failure.','Time assumptions, shared mutable state, random data, external dependencies and race conditions.'],['Do not add unlimited test retries.','Do not delete assertions to make tests stable.'],'CI quality is itself a production engineering concern because delivery decisions depend on it.'),
lesson('deep-046','L3','GitHub Actions Security','Why pin actions by commit SHA?','Pinning an action to an immutable commit reduces the risk that a mutable tag later points to unexpected code.','Use trusted action versions pinned to reviewed SHAs and update them intentionally through dependency maintenance.','uses: actions/checkout@<reviewed-sha>','I treat CI actions as executable supply-chain dependencies with repository privileges, so provenance and review matter.',['Is a tag enough?','What else should a workflow restrict?'],['A tag can move; a commit SHA is immutable for the referenced object, though trust in the repository still matters.','Permissions, token scopes, environments, secrets exposure and untrusted pull-request code paths.'],['Do not grant write-all permissions by default.','Do not expose production secrets to forked PRs.'],'The workflow itself is part of the production attack surface.'),
lesson('deep-047','L3','Jenkins Security','Why should Jenkins credentials be scoped?','Credentials should be available only to the jobs/stages and identities that require them, limiting blast radius.','Use credential IDs, folder/job permissions, dedicated service identities and short-lived credentials where possible. Avoid printing credential values.','withCredentials([string(credentialsId:"deploy-token", variable:"TOKEN")]) { ... }','I design credentials around least privilege and lifecycle, not convenience. A deployment token should not also administer the whole cloud account.',['Can Jenkins mask every secret?','How do you protect production deploys?'],['Masking reduces accidental log exposure but cannot protect a secret that a malicious build can deliberately exfiltrate.','Use restricted jobs, approvals/environments, scoped roles and separate deployment identities.'],['Do not inject secrets into untrusted build steps.','Do not reuse one administrator credential across pipelines.'],'CI trust boundaries must account for who can change pipeline code.'),
lesson('deep-048','L3','Cloud IAM','Why is workload identity better than static access keys?','Workload identity lets a running workload obtain credentials through an identity mechanism instead of storing a long-lived secret in the application.','Bind a Kubernetes service account or CI identity to a narrowly scoped cloud role, issue temporary credentials and audit role use.','Pod identity → STS → temporary credentials\nS3:GetObject on bucket/prefix only','I want the workload to prove who it is at runtime, then receive only the permissions required for that action.',['What if the Pod is compromised?','Should all services share one role?'],['The attacker may gain the workload’s permissions, which is why least privilege and segmentation limit blast radius.','No. Separate identities make access auditable and reduce the impact of compromise.'],['Do not give a frontend workload cloud write permissions.','Do not use one shared “app-admin” role for every service.'],'IAM is a runtime architecture decision, not just an infrastructure setting.'),
lesson('deep-049','L3','Full-Stack Architecture','Where should validation happen in React + Spring Boot?','Client validation improves feedback; server validation is authoritative because requests can bypass the browser.','Share conceptual rules where useful, but enforce security and business invariants on the server and return structured validation errors for the UI.','React: required field + format\nSpring: @Valid + business rule\nDB: constraints for durable invariants','I use layered validation: UX checks early, API checks authoritatively, and database constraints protect durable invariants.',['Why validate in the DB too?','How should errors be returned?'],['A race or alternate write path can bypass application checks; constraints protect critical data invariants.','Use stable machine-readable fields plus human-readable messages so React can map errors to inputs.'],['Do not rely on client validation for security.','Do not duplicate complex business rules in JavaScript and assume they stay identical forever.'],'Validation belongs at boundaries appropriate to the invariant.'),
lesson('deep-050','L3','Full-Stack Architecture','How should a React app handle access-token expiry?','The client should detect an expired/invalid session, attempt a controlled refresh if the architecture supports it, then redirect or show re-authentication when refresh fails.','Centralize API handling so 401 responses are processed consistently; avoid multiple simultaneous refresh requests and replay only safe/idempotent requests.','API call → 401 → single refresh → retry original\nrefresh fails → sign in','I treat token refresh as a concurrency problem as well as an authentication problem. Multiple components should not race to refresh the same session.',['Where should refresh tokens live?','Should every failed request retry automatically?'],['Prefer secure, appropriately scoped mechanisms such as HttpOnly cookies where the architecture supports them.','Only when the failure is genuinely due to expired authentication and replay is safe under the API contract.'],['Do not infinite-loop on repeated 401 responses.','Do not store long-lived secrets in unsafe browser storage without understanding the threat model.'],'Centralized API clients simplify authentication lifecycle handling.'),
lesson('deep-051','L3','Full-Stack Data','How do you prevent an N+1 query from a REST endpoint?','N+1 occurs when loading a collection causes one query for the collection plus one query per item for a related association.','Use fetch joins/entity graphs/projections or batch fetching based on the response shape, then verify SQL count and execution plan.','orders: 1 query\nitems: +N queries ❌\nfetch plan/projection → bounded queries ✓','I diagnose N+1 from actual SQL, not from annotations alone. The right fix depends on whether the endpoint truly needs the related data.',['Is eager loading always the fix?','How do you prove N+1 is gone?'],['No. Global eager loading can create huge joins and unnecessary data; shape-specific fetching is safer.','Enable SQL/ORM statistics or inspect traces and count database calls for a representative request.'],['Do not make every relationship EAGER.','Do not solve N+1 by returning giant object graphs.'],'API response design and persistence fetch strategy should be considered together.'),
lesson('deep-052','L3','Full-Stack Data','Why should API DTOs be separate from JPA entities?','DTOs define an external contract while entities model persistence state and relationships. Coupling them can leak fields, trigger lazy loads and make database changes break clients.','Map request/response DTOs explicitly and control which fields are writable/readable. Use projections where appropriate for read models.','OrderEntity → OrderResponse\n{id,status,total}\nnot entity graph directly','I use DTOs to make the API contract intentional. Persistence models can evolve internally without exposing every relationship and field.',['Does mapping add overhead?','Can records be used for DTOs?'],['Mapping has cost, but it buys contract isolation and often avoids accidental data loading; measure if it is significant.','Yes, Java records are useful for immutable request/response DTOs when their semantics fit.'],['Do not expose password hashes or internal flags through entity serialization.','Do not bind arbitrary JSON directly to entities for privileged updates.'],'DTO boundaries are especially valuable in long-lived APIs.'),
lesson('deep-053','L3','Full-Stack Caching','Where should caching happen in a full-stack system?','Caching can exist in the browser, CDN, API layer, application cache or database, and each layer has different freshness and invalidation semantics.','Cache stable public assets aggressively, use HTTP cache validators for API resources where appropriate, and use application caches only when ownership and invalidation are explicit.','Browser/CDN → HTTP cache\nAPI → Redis\nDB → buffer/cache','I choose the cache closest to the repeated expensive work while preserving correctness. The hardest part is usually invalidation, not storage.',['When is Redis unnecessary?','What is ETag useful for?'],['If data is cheap to compute and correctness/freshness are more important, adding Redis adds operational complexity without value.','It lets a client validate whether a representation changed and avoid retransmitting the full body when it has not.'],['Do not cache authorization-sensitive data under shared keys.','Do not assume TTL alone guarantees acceptable freshness.'],'Caching should have a clear hit target, invalidation rule and failure behavior.'),
lesson('deep-054','L3','Full-Stack Uploads','How should large file uploads be designed?','Large uploads should avoid routing all bytes through an application server when direct object storage upload is practical.','The API authorizes the upload and issues a short-lived signed URL; the browser uploads directly to storage; the API receives/records completion metadata.','React → API: create upload\nAPI → signed URL\nReact → object storage\nAPI ← completion','I keep authorization in the API but move bulk data transfer to storage infrastructure to reduce application bandwidth and memory pressure.',['How do you validate file type?','What about malware scanning?'],['Validate metadata and actual content where needed; never trust only a filename or Content-Type supplied by the client.','Store/quarantine the object, scan asynchronously, then mark it available only after the scan passes.'],['Do not accept arbitrary object keys from clients.','Do not make uploaded content publicly executable by default.'],'Upload architecture is a security and scalability problem together.'),
lesson('deep-055','L3','Resilience Architecture','What is a circuit breaker protecting?','A circuit breaker stops repeatedly calling a failing dependency after a threshold, giving the dependency and caller a chance to recover.','Closed allows calls, open fails fast, half-open probes recovery. Pair it with timeouts and bounded concurrency; otherwise queued calls can still overload the system.','CLOSED → failures → OPEN\nwait → HALF_OPEN\nsuccess → CLOSED','I use circuit breakers to control failure propagation, not to hide every error. The fallback must be semantically safe.',['When should a breaker open?','What is a bad fallback?'],['After measured failures/latency indicate the dependency is unhealthy according to configured thresholds.','Returning fabricated success or stale data when correctness is required.'],['Do not wrap every local method in a circuit breaker.','Do not use a fallback that violates business invariants.'],'Circuit breakers are one component of a broader resilience budget.'),
lesson('deep-056','L3','Release Engineering','How do blue-green and canary deployments differ?','Blue-green switches traffic between two environments; canary gradually shifts a subset of traffic to the new version while observing behavior.','Blue-green offers a relatively simple cutover/rollback path but doubles environment capacity; canary reduces exposure but needs traffic control and good telemetry.','blue → 100%\ncanary → 5% → 25% → 50% → 100%','I choose the strategy based on blast radius, rollback speed, state compatibility and traffic-control capability.',['Can you canary database schema changes?','Why can blue-green still fail?'],['Use backward-compatible expand/contract migrations so both versions can operate during the transition.','Shared databases, incompatible schemas or external side effects can make rollback unsafe.'],['Do not treat traffic percentage alone as a safe canary.','Do not make destructive schema changes before old code is gone.'],'Release safety depends on compatibility as much as deployment mechanics.'),
lesson('deep-057','L3','Database Migrations','How should zero-downtime schema changes work?','Schema changes should be introduced in stages so old and new application versions can coexist during rollout.','Use expand-and-contract: add compatible structures, deploy code that can use both, backfill, switch reads/writes, then remove obsolete structures later.','1 add nullable column\n2 deploy dual-write\n3 backfill\n4 read new\n5 remove old','I design schema changes for mixed-version operation because rolling and canary deployments create overlap by default.',['Why not rename the column directly?','How do you backfill safely?'],['Old Pods may still reference the old name, causing failures during rollout.','Batch work, rate-limit it, monitor locks/load and make it restartable.'],['Do not combine destructive DDL with the first application deployment.','Do not run an unbounded backfill inside one giant transaction.'],'Database compatibility is a prerequisite for safe deployment strategies.'),
lesson('deep-058','L3','Kubernetes Security','How do you reduce Kubernetes Pod privilege?','Pod security should limit filesystem, Linux capabilities, privilege escalation, identity and network reachability.','Use non-root users, read-only root filesystems where possible, drop capabilities, seccomp, service-account minimization and NetworkPolicies.','securityContext:\n  runAsNonRoot: true\n  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true','I apply least privilege at multiple layers and verify the application actually works with the restrictions.',['Why is read-only root filesystem useful?','What does NetworkPolicy add?'],['It prevents many classes of persistence/tampering and forces writable state into explicit volumes/tmpfs.','It restricts allowed Pod network flows according to policy and selected traffic rules.'],['Do not solve permission errors by enabling privileged mode.','Do not assume a NetworkPolicy exists just because the cluster supports it.'],'Security controls are strongest when they are explicit and testable in deployment manifests.'),
lesson('deep-059','L3','Incident Response','What should a production incident timeline contain?','A timeline records observable events, decisions, actions and outcomes with timestamps so responders can coordinate and later learn from the incident.','Capture alerts, symptom onset, mitigation, configuration/deployment changes, key hypotheses, evidence and recovery. Keep facts separate from guesses.','14:02 alert\n14:05 rollback started\n14:08 error rate normal\n14:20 root cause confirmed','I use the timeline to preserve shared context during pressure. It should help the next responder understand what happened without relying on memory.',['Why separate facts from hypotheses?','What belongs in a postmortem?'],['Unverified assumptions can become “facts” and send responders down the wrong path.','Impact, timeline, root cause/contributing factors, detection, response, corrective actions and owners.'],['Do not blame individuals in the timeline.','Do not rewrite history after recovery to make it look cleaner.'],'Incident documentation is an operational tool, not bureaucracy.'),
lesson('deep-060','L3','Disaster Recovery','How do RTO and RPO influence architecture?','RTO is how quickly service should be restored; RPO is how much recent data loss is acceptable.','Tighter targets require stronger replication, backups, automation, tested failover and sometimes multi-region design.','RPO=5m → replication/backup strategy\nRTO=30m → automated restore/runbook','I start from business impact and derive technical controls. A multi-region system is not justified by “high availability” alone; it must satisfy a defined recovery target.',['Can backups alone meet a 5-minute RPO?','How do you validate DR?'],['Usually not unless backup frequency and completion guarantees support it; continuous replication or log shipping may be required.','Perform restore/failover exercises and measure actual recovery time and data loss against targets.'],['Do not call an untested backup a disaster-recovery solution.','Do not confuse HA with DR.'],'Recovery objectives should be measurable and regularly tested.'),
lesson('deep-061','L3','Architecture Integration','How should a request ID travel across the stack?','A correlation identifier links logs and traces for one request across browser, gateway, Spring services and downstream calls.','Generate or accept a safe identifier at the edge, validate length/format, propagate it in headers, include it in structured logs and trace attributes.','X-Request-ID: 8f2a...\nReact → ALB → Spring → Kafka header','I use request IDs for correlation, while distributed traces provide richer timing. Never treat a client-supplied ID as trusted identity.',['Request ID vs trace ID?','Should the server accept any request ID?'],['Trace ID belongs to the tracing model; request ID can be a simpler application correlation field.','It can accept a bounded safe value or replace it; it must never become an authorization credential.'],['Do not log unbounded user input as identifiers.','Do not put sensitive information into IDs.'],'Consistent correlation dramatically reduces cross-service debugging time.'),
lesson('deep-062','L3','Architecture Integration','How should Kafka and PostgreSQL work together without dual writes?','The database transaction should own the state change and an outbox record, avoiding a race where one succeeds and the other fails.','Commit business data plus outbox in one local transaction. A relay publishes events and marks them delivered; consumers are idempotent.','BEGIN\nUPDATE orders...\nINSERT outbox...\nCOMMIT\nrelay → Kafka','I use the database as the source of truth for the state change and make event publication a reliable follow-up step.',['What if the relay crashes after publishing?','How do you prevent outbox growth?'],['The event may be published again, so consumers need idempotency; delivery status alone cannot prove Kafka has no duplicate.','Publish reliably, mark/compact delivered records and archive/delete them according to retention and replay needs.'],['Do not delete the outbox before durable publication.','Do not make consumers assume exactly one delivery.'],'This pattern is one of the most useful bridges between transactional data and asynchronous architecture.'),
lesson('deep-063','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you propagate deadlines across React, Spring Boot and downstream services?','A request deadline is the maximum useful time remaining for the user operation, and downstream calls should respect the remaining budget.','Set client/server timeouts, derive per-call deadlines, propagate timeout metadata where supported, and cancel work that can no longer contribute to the response.','user budget 2s\nAPI 1.8s\nDB 700ms\nHTTP downstream 800ms max','I avoid independent five-second timeouts at every layer. The whole request has one budget that gets smaller as work progresses.',['What happens when the budget expires?','Why not just increase timeouts?'],['Stop or fail the request and release resources promptly; asynchronous work should be separated if it still has value.','Longer waits consume threads/connections and can turn a dependency slowdown into system-wide saturation.'],['Do not let child calls outlive the user request without a deliberate async design.','Do not multiply timeout values across layers.'],'Deadline propagation is a practical way to control cascading latency.'),
lesson('deep-064','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you reason about a full-stack 503 during a traffic spike?','A 503 indicates the request path has no usable capacity at some layer; the correct diagnosis depends on where capacity was exhausted.','Compare edge target health, Kubernetes endpoints, Pod readiness, HPA, CPU/memory, thread pools, DB pool usage, Kafka lag and downstream errors.','traffic ↑ → pods ↑ → DB connections maxed\n→ API latency ↑ → 503','I look for the first saturated resource. Scaling the API may worsen the outage if the database is already at its connection or CPU limit.',['Why can more Pods make it worse?','What metric proves the bottleneck?'],['Each Pod may add database connections, cache load or downstream concurrency, increasing pressure on the constrained dependency.','Evidence such as DB active connections/CPU, pool pending threads and trace latency should align with the symptom.'],['Do not scale every deployment blindly.','Do not disable readiness to keep unhealthy Pods in rotation.'],'Capacity is a graph of shared constraints, not a single CPU number.'),
lesson('deep-065','L3','Architecture Integration','How should configuration move from source to production?','Application code should remain environment-independent while deployment supplies validated configuration and secrets at runtime.','Build one artifact, inject non-secret config through deployment config and secrets through a secret manager/workload identity, validate required values at startup.','image: same\nstaging env → config A\nprod env → config B','I keep configuration separate from artifacts so the same build can be promoted safely and secrets can rotate without rebuilding code.',['What should fail at startup?','Where should defaults live?'],['Missing security-critical or required connection settings should fail fast rather than start a partially functional service.','Safe local defaults can exist in code, but production-sensitive values should be explicit and environment-controlled.'],['Do not silently default production credentials or endpoints.','Do not commit secret configuration “for convenience.”'],'Configuration drift becomes easier to control when artifacts are immutable.'),
lesson('deep-066','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you make graceful shutdown work across Kubernetes and Spring Boot?','Graceful shutdown stops accepting new work and allows in-flight requests to finish before the process exits, within a bounded termination window.','Kubernetes sends termination, readiness changes, endpoints update, the application stops accepting new requests and waits for active work before the grace period expires.','SIGTERM → not ready → drain → finish requests → exit','I design shutdown as a protocol across Kubernetes, load balancers and the application, not just a JVM setting.',['Why can requests still arrive after SIGTERM?','What if shutdown takes too long?'],['Traffic propagation takes time and in-flight connections may still exist, so the grace period and load balancer behavior matter.','Force termination after a bounded grace period; investigate slow requests and make work resumable where necessary.'],['Do not use infinite shutdown waits.','Do not rely on application shutdown alone without readiness/endpoint draining.'],'Graceful termination is essential for zero-downtime rolling deployments.'),
lesson('deep-067','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you debug a frontend “API is down” report?','The browser symptom may originate from DNS, TLS, CORS, authentication, routing, server errors or a backend dependency.','Inspect the exact Network request, status, timing and response headers/body, reproduce with curl when possible, then trace the request into backend logs.','Browser Network → request URL/status → curl → gateway → Spring logs → downstream','I start from the exact failed request rather than the user’s summary. “API is down” is a symptom, not a diagnosis.',['What if there is no request in Network?','What if Network shows 200 but UI is broken?'],['The failure may be client-side before fetch, routing code, service worker or an event handler.','Inspect response shape, parsing, state updates and rendering; the transport can be healthy while application logic is wrong.'],['Do not restart backend infrastructure for a client-only error.','Do not trust only the status code; inspect timing and payload.'],'This is the practical “first failing boundary” method.'),
lesson('deep-068','L3','Architecture Integration','How should a production health endpoint be designed?','Health endpoints should expose enough information for orchestration and operators without becoming an expensive dependency fan-out or data leak.','Separate liveness from readiness, keep checks bounded, include dependency state only where it determines readiness, and protect detailed diagnostics.','/actuator/health/liveness\n/actuator/health/readiness','I design health around action: should the process be restarted, or should it receive traffic? Detailed diagnostics belong in controlled observability, not public endpoints.',['Should readiness query every dependency?','Should health return database credentials?'],['Only dependencies whose unavailability means this instance cannot safely serve traffic; keep checks cheap and bounded.','Never. Return safe status information and keep sensitive connection details private.'],['Do not make liveness fail because Kafka is unavailable.','Do not expose stack traces or connection strings publicly.'],'Health semantics should reduce automated harm rather than create restart storms.'),
lesson('deep-069','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you connect logs to a deployment version?','Every observable signal should identify the application version/build so behavior can be correlated with releases.','Inject commit SHA/image digest/build version into application metadata, structured logs, metrics labels where cardinality permits, and trace resource attributes.','version=2026.09.15-abc123\nimage=sha256:...','I want an incident responder to answer “what code is serving this request?” from telemetry and deployment metadata.',['Why prefer image digest?','Should commit SHA be a metric label?'],['A digest identifies the exact image bytes; tags can be moved.','Only if the number of versions is bounded enough for the metric system; otherwise keep version in logs/traces or deployment metadata.'],['Do not rely on latest as incident evidence.','Do not create unbounded metric cardinality with request IDs.'],'Traceability links delivery engineering to observability.'),
lesson('deep-070','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you design a safe rollback?','Rollback means restoring a known-good application state while preserving or reconciling data changes and external side effects.','Keep previous artifacts available, make schemas backward compatible, define traffic cutback, verify health/SLOs and understand irreversible side effects.','new version unhealthy\n→ shift traffic to old\n→ verify\n→ investigate\n→ forward-fix if schema changed','I never equate “kubectl rollout undo” with a complete rollback plan. The application, database and external side effects must all be considered.',['When is forward-fix safer?','What makes rollback impossible?'],['When the new schema or data format is not compatible with the old version, deploying a corrective version may be safer.','Destructive migrations, irreversible external actions and incompatible state changes can prevent safe code rollback.'],['Do not test rollback for the first time during a major incident.','Do not delete the previous artifact immediately after release.'],'Rollback readiness is part of release readiness.'),
lesson('deep-071','L3','Architecture Integration','How should Redis caching behave when Redis is unavailable?','A cache should usually be an optimization whose failure does not automatically become total application failure, unless the business explicitly requires it.','Use bounded timeouts, graceful fallback to the source of truth, stampede protection and clear behavior for writes/invalidation.','GET product → Redis miss/error → PostgreSQL → response','I design cache failure as a dependency failure scenario. If Redis is optional, the API should degrade predictably instead of waiting indefinitely.',['When can Redis be a hard dependency?','How do you prevent cache stampede?'],['When the architecture explicitly uses it as the authoritative state or required coordination mechanism, with appropriate durability/HA.','Request coalescing, locks, jittered expiry or prewarming depending on workload.'],['Do not retry Redis indefinitely.','Do not cache authorization decisions without an explicit invalidation model.'],'Every cache needs a failure mode, not only a hit path.'),
lesson('deep-072','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you protect a Spring Boot service from a slow downstream API?','Use deadlines, bounded concurrency, circuit breaking and an explicit fallback or failure response so slow dependencies cannot consume all local capacity.','Configure HTTP client connection/read timeouts, limit concurrent calls, propagate deadlines, record dependency latency and fail fast when the budget is exhausted.','threads=100\ndownstream hangs\n→ all 100 blocked\n→ request queue grows','I protect local capacity first. A downstream timeout is useful only if it also releases threads and connections before the service saturates.',['Why is connection timeout different from read timeout?','What if there is no safe fallback?'],['Connection timeout bounds establishing the connection; read timeout bounds waiting for response data.','Fail explicitly with an appropriate status/error rather than fabricate success; resilience does not require pretending the operation succeeded.'],['Do not set only a connection timeout.','Do not use fallback data when correctness matters more than availability.'],'Downstream protection is a local capacity problem as much as a network problem.'),
lesson('deep-073','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you design an audit trail for sensitive changes?','An audit trail records who changed what, when, from which request/context, and the resulting action in a tamper-resistant manner.','Capture actor identity, action, resource, timestamp, request/correlation ID and relevant before/after metadata while excluding secrets. Store with controlled retention and access.','actor=42 action=ROLE_CHANGED resource=user:7 trace=abc123','I distinguish audit records from debug logs: audit data has business/legal meaning and therefore needs stronger integrity, retention and access controls.',['Should audit logs be editable?','What should not be recorded?'],['Access should be tightly controlled and direct mutation restricted; append-oriented storage is preferable.','Passwords, tokens, private keys and unnecessary sensitive payloads.'],['Do not treat ordinary application logs as the only audit trail.','Do not log complete request bodies by default.'],'Security-sensitive systems often need both operational logs and a durable audit history.'),
lesson('deep-074','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you handle duplicate Kafka events safely in Spring Boot?','A consumer must assume duplicates can happen and make the business effect idempotent.','Use a stable event ID and persist processed IDs or enforce a unique business constraint in the same local transaction as the side effect.','eventId=evt-123\nINSERT processed_event(event_id)\nUPDATE order ...','I make duplicate handling part of the consumer contract. A successful first processing and a repeated delivery should converge to the same state.',['Where should deduplication live?','What if the database transaction fails?'],['At the consumer’s durable side-effect boundary, usually the same database transaction as the business change.','Do not mark the event processed; allow redelivery after rollback.'],['Do not store deduplication only in process memory.','Do not acknowledge permanently before the durable side effect succeeds.'],'Idempotent consumers make retries safe.'),
lesson('deep-075','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you reason about a thread-pool exhaustion incident?','Thread-pool exhaustion occurs when work occupies all available execution threads, causing new tasks to queue or time out.','Inspect active/queued counts, task latency, blocking calls, downstream timeouts and pool configuration. Separate CPU-bound and blocking workloads when appropriate.','pool=200\nactive=200\nqueue=5000\ndownstream p99=8s','I look for what holds threads, not just the pool size. Increasing threads can worsen memory, context switching and downstream pressure.',['What does a large queue indicate?','How can async code still exhaust resources?'],['Arrival exceeds service capacity or tasks are blocked for too long.','Async stages still consume executor threads, connection pools and downstream resources when they perform blocking work.'],['Do not increase pool size blindly.','Do not use unbounded queues for latency-sensitive requests.'],'Thread pools, DB pools and connection pools form a coupled capacity system.'),
lesson('deep-076','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you design rate limiting for a public API?','Rate limiting bounds request volume per identity, API key, IP or resource to protect capacity and enforce fair use.','Choose a policy such as token bucket, define burst/rate limits, return 429 with retry guidance, and place limits at an edge where possible while preserving business-specific controls.','token bucket: capacity=100\nrefill=20/s\nrequest consumes 1','I distinguish abuse protection from capacity management. The limit should be tied to a known service budget and identity model.',['Where should rate limiting happen?','Can one global limit work for all endpoints?'],['At the edge for broad protection, with service-level limits for expensive or sensitive operations.','Usually not; login, search and file export have very different cost profiles.'],['Do not rate-limit only by IP for authenticated systems behind NAT.','Do not return 500 when the policy intentionally rejects excess traffic.'],'Rate limiting is an admission-control mechanism.'),
lesson('deep-077','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you make a scheduled job safe across multiple Kubernetes replicas?','A horizontally scaled application may run the same scheduled trigger in every Pod unless leadership or distributed coordination prevents duplicate execution.','Use a dedicated Job/CronJob, leader election, a durable distributed lock, or an idempotent job design depending on the requirement.','CronJob → Job → worker\nnot every API Pod → same task','I first decide whether duplicate execution is harmless. If not, I use an explicit coordination mechanism rather than hoping replicas line up.',['Why can @Scheduled be dangerous in replicas?','Is a distributed lock always necessary?'],['Each application instance can execute its own scheduler.','No, if the work is idempotent or a dedicated single-runner mechanism exists.'],['Do not assume one JVM scheduler means one cluster execution.','Do not build a fragile lock without lease/expiry semantics.'],'Scheduled work needs the same distributed-systems thinking as request processing.'),
lesson('deep-078','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you design API error contracts that survive frontend evolution?','An error contract should expose stable machine-readable codes and contextual fields while allowing human messages to evolve.','Return a consistent envelope with status, code, message, correlation ID and field errors where relevant. Version semantics, not wording, when necessary.','{\n "code":"ORDER_NOT_FOUND",\n "message":"Order not found",\n "requestId":"abc"\n}','I make the code stable for React and keep message text suitable for humans. That prevents UI logic from depending on exact English strings.',['Should frontend branch on status text?','What belongs in validation errors?'],['No. Use stable error codes and structured fields.','Field/path, rule code and safe message; never expose stack traces or internal SQL details.'],['Do not return Java exception class names as API contracts.','Do not let error messages reveal sensitive implementation details.'],'Stable contracts reduce coupling between frontend and backend release cycles.'),
lesson('deep-079','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you test a full-stack change without relying only on mocks?','A reliable test strategy uses unit tests for local logic plus integration and end-to-end tests at real boundaries where contract failures matter.','Use Testcontainers or equivalent for database/broker integration, API contract tests, and a small number of browser flows for critical user journeys.','unit → service\nintegration → PostgreSQL/Kafka\ne2e → React + API','I test the boundaries that mocks cannot prove: serialization, SQL behavior, security filters, transactions, messaging and browser integration.',['Why not use only end-to-end tests?','Where do mocks help?'],['They are slower and less diagnostic; a layered suite gives faster feedback and isolates failures.','For deterministic local collaborators and rare external behavior where a real dependency would be costly.'],['Do not mock the database to prove SQL correctness.','Do not build hundreds of brittle browser tests for every branch.'],'Testing strategy should mirror architectural boundaries.'),
lesson('deep-080','L3','Architecture Integration','How do you turn a technical design into an interview answer?','A strong answer connects requirements, constraints, architecture, data flow, failure modes and trade-offs rather than listing technologies.','Start with requirements, draw the request/data path, choose components with reasons, explain scaling/consistency/security/observability, then discuss one failure and one trade-off.','requirements → boundaries → data flow → scale → failure → trade-off','I explain the system as a sequence of decisions. That lets me answer follow-ups because each choice has a reason and an explicit constraint.',['What should you say when unsure?','How deep should you go?'],['State the assumption, explain the trade-off and avoid inventing certainty. A reasoned assumption is stronger than a confident guess.','Match depth to the interviewer’s question, then go one level deeper into internals or failure handling when useful.'],['Do not recite a memorized architecture diagram.','Do not name five technologies without explaining why they solve the constraint.'],'The goal is understanding deep enough to derive the answer under pressure.')
];
